WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:08.444
You've probably seen all sorts of items&nbsp;flying around recently, and it can get a little confusing as to how they all relate to&nbsp;one another.

00:00:08.444 --> 00:00:15.310
Machine learning, deep learning, foundation models. And you've probably seen&nbsp;other terms like generative AI and large language models.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:20.400
So let's bring an end to the&nbsp;confusion and put these terms in their place.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.246
There's one thing they all have in common.

00:00:24.246 --> 00:00:30.444
They are all terms related to the field of artificial intelligence or A.I.

00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:37.444
Now A.I.&nbsp;refers to the simulation of human intelligence in machines enabling them to perform tasks that typically require human thinking.

00:00:37.444 --> 00:00:42.884
Now and its various forms and paradigms has&nbsp;been around for decades.

00:00:42.884 --> 00:00:54.746
Perhaps you've heard of the chat bot called Eliza, that&nbsp;was developed in the mid 1960s, and that&nbsp;&nbsp; could mimic human like conversation, to an extent.

00:00:54.746 --> 00:01:06.786
Now a subfield of A.I. is called machine learning, so this sits within the field of AI. Now what's machine learning?

00:01:06.786 --> 00:01:13.786
Well, it focuses on developing algorithms that allow computers to learn from and&nbsp;make decisions based upon data,

00:01:13.786 --> 00:01:19.923
rather than being explicitly programed to perform a specific task.

00:01:19.923 --> 00:01:27.360
These algorithms use statistical techniques to learn patterns in data and make predictions&nbsp;or decisions without human intervention.&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:35.592
But like A.I. ML or machine learning is a very&nbsp;broad term. It encompasses a range of techniques and approaches

00:01:35.592 --> 00:01:42.592
from traditional statistical&nbsp;methods through to complex neural networks. Now, some of the core categories within ML we can&nbsp;think of are firstly supervised learning,

00:01:42.592 --> 00:01:53.881
where models are trained on labeled data. There's also&nbsp;unsupervised learning,

00:01:53.881 --> 00:02:00.881
and that's where the models find patterns in data without predefined labels. And there's also reinforcement learning.

00:02:00.881 --> 00:02:07.881
And that's where models learn by interacting with an&nbsp;environment and receiving feedback.

00:02:07.881 --> 00:02:20.485
Okay, so where does deep learning come in? Well, deep&nbsp;learning is a subset of machine learning.

00:02:20.485 --> 00:02:28.740
Goes right there. Now, that specifically focuses on&nbsp;artificial neural networks with multiple layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:37.500
and we can think of them looking bit like this. So these are nodes and all of our connections.&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:44.500
Now, those layers where we get the deep part&nbsp;from. And while traditional ML techniques might be efficient for linear separations&nbsp;or simpler patterns,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:51.500
deep learning excels at handling vast amounts of unstructured data&nbsp;like images or natural language

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.287
and discovering intricate structures within them.

00:02:56.287 --> 00:03:07.020
Now, I do want&nbsp;to point out that not all machine learning is deep learning. Traditional machine learning methods&nbsp;still play a pivotal role in many applications.&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:19.574
So we've got techniques like linear regression,&nbsp;that's a popular technique, or decision trees, or support vector machines, or clustering algorithms.

00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:29.280
These are all other types of machine learning, and they've been widely used for a long time. In some&nbsp;scenarios, look, deep learning might be overkill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.975
or it just isn't the most suitable approach. Okay,&nbsp;so machine learning, deep learning,

00:03:34.975 --> 00:03:42.335
what else ah, yeah, foundation models. Okay, so where&nbsp;do foundation models fit into this?

00:03:42.335 --> 00:03:47.961
Well, the term foundation model was popularized in 2021&nbsp;by researchers at the Stanford Institute

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:58.740
and it fits primarily within the realm of deep learning.&nbsp;So I'm going to put foundation models right here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:10.920
Now, these models are large scale neural&nbsp;networks trained on vast amounts of data,&nbsp;and they serve as a base or a foundation for a multitude of applications.&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:17.920
So instead of training a model from scratch for&nbsp;each specific task, you can take a Pre-trained&nbsp;foundation model and fine tune it for&nbsp;a particular application,

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:26.100
which saves a bunch of time and resources. Now, foundation&nbsp;models have been trained on diverse datasets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:34.207
capturing a broad range of knowledge and can be&nbsp;adapted to tasks ranging from language translation&nbsp;to content generation to image recognition.

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:42.720
So in the grand scheme of things foundation models, they sit within the deep learning category&nbsp;but represent a shift towards more generalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.871
adaptable and scalable AI solutions.

00:04:45.871 --> 00:04:52.871
So look,&nbsp;I think this is hopefully looking a bit clearer now. But there are some other A.I. related&nbsp;terms. I think it's worth also explaining.

00:04:52.871 --> 00:05:01.708
And one of those is large language models or LLMs Now,

00:05:01.708 --> 00:05:06.365
these are a specific type of foundation model, so I've put them in this box here,

00:05:06.365 --> 00:05:13.030
and they&nbsp;are centered around processing and generating humanlike text. So let's break it down, LLM.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:22.800
The first L that's large, and that refers to the scale of the model. LLMs possess a vast number&nbsp;of parameters, often in the billions or even more.&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.440
And this enormity is part of what gives LLMs&nbsp;their nuanced understanding and capability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:35.184
Second, L that language that designed to&nbsp;understand and interact using human languages,

00:05:35.184 --> 00:05:42.856
as they are trained on massive data sets. LLMs can&nbsp;grasp grammar, context, idioms and even cultural&nbsp;references. 

00:05:42.856 --> 00:05:47.655
And the last letter and that's for&nbsp;model at the core that computational models

00:05:47.655 --> 00:05:53.100
a series of algorithms and parameters working&nbsp;
together to process input and produce output.&nbsp;

00:05:53.100 --> 00:06:00.260
LLMs can handle a broad spectrum of language&nbsp;tasks like answering questions, translating or even creative writing.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:07.997
Now, if LLMs one example&nbsp;of foundation models, what are some others? Well, there's a bunch we can think of.

00:06:07.997 --> 00:06:18.000
One of those is&nbsp;being vision models that can see in and in quotes, interpret and generate images. There are&nbsp;scientific models.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:26.456
Give that an S and scientific models, for example, are used in biology where&nbsp;there are models for predicting how proteins fold into 3D shapes,

00:06:26.456 --> 00:06:35.848
and there are audio models&nbsp;as well for generating human sounding speech or composing the next fake Drake hit song.

00:06:35.848 --> 00:06:45.360
And finally, one last term that's gaining traction. We've all heard&nbsp;about it. It's generative AI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:53.040
Now this term pertains to models and algorithms&nbsp;specifically crafted to generate new content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.540
Essentially, while foundation models provide&nbsp;the underlying structure and understanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.075
generative AI is about harnessing that knowledge&nbsp;to produce something that is new.

00:07:02.075 --> 00:07:09.600
It's the creative expression that emerges from the vast&nbsp;knowledge base of these foundation models.&nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:16.708
And with that, I think we've fully filled&nbsp;out a AI buzzword bingo scorecard.

00:07:16.708 --> 00:07:22.140
And look, we have detailed videos on all of these&nbsp;topics. So check those out to learn more.

